<p>The population influx associated with the new Standard Oil Company refinery had many effects on Mandan. Membership in <a href="/places/landmarks/saint-joseph-church/">St. Joseph's Church</a> parish reached more than 6,500 in 1958–1959, an unheard-of size for the region and an unworkable number for effective pastoral ministry. It was time for Mandan to establish a second Catholic parish.</p>

<p>St. Joseph's pastor {% person "aloyius-galowitsch" %}Aloyius Galowitsch{% endperson %} held a kickoff meeting on August 8, 1957. He asked members of the community to donate one day's wages per month for 30 months. Land in northwest Mandan was acquired from {% person "william-neff" %}Dr. William Neff{% endperson %} and {% person "clem-albers" %}Clem Albers{% endperson %}. {% person "bernard-hillyer" %}Bernard Hillyer{% endperson %} of Bismarck, who designed many churches in the diocese, was selected as project architect. Lund Construction won the contract for the church and school.</p>

